.TH CCOL_DETERMINE_CCOL_DATA_TYPE 3 "2026-10-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_determine_ccol_data_type, ccol_data_type \- the data type enumerator
that matches the type of an expression
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/common.h>

typedef enum ccollections_data_type {
  ccol_char = 0,
  ccol_short = 1,
  ccol_int = 2,
  ccol_long = 3,
  ccol_long_long = 4,
  ccol_unsigned_char = 5,
  ccol_unsigned_short = 6,
  ccol_unsigned_int = 7,
  ccol_unsigned_long = 8,
  ccol_unsigned_long_long = 9,
  ccol_float = 10,
  ccol_double = 11,
  ccol_long_double = 12,
  ccol_pointer = 13,
  ccol_string = 14,
  ccol_other_types = 15,
  ccol_signed_char = 16,
} ccol_data_type;

ccol_data_type ccol_determine_ccol_data_type(data);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
ccol_data_type names the type of the keys or the values of a map or a
cache. chmap_create(3), chmap_create_mp(3), chmap_create_ch(3) and
chmap_create_full(3) take one for the keys (key_type) and one for the
values (val_type). cbmap_create(3), cbmap_create_mp(3),
cbmap_create_ch(3) and cbmap_create_full(3) take one for the keys, and
clrucache_create_full(3) takes one for the keys and one for the values.
The enumerator decides how the container stores, compares and hashes an
element, and which width a cmap_pair of a fixed-width type must carry
(see ccol_fixed_width_data_type_size(3)).

ccol_determine_ccol_data_type() returns the enumerator that matches the
type of data. It looks only at the type, at compile time, so the result
does not depend on the value that data holds. A caller of the raw create
functions uses it to get the right value. The type-inferred construct
macros, such as chmap_construct(3) and cbmap_construct(3), apply the
same rule to their declared types, so a map made through either layer
treats its elements the same way.

The enumerators stand for the C types below, and each one also covers
the const-qualified spelling of its type:

  ccol_char                char
  ccol_signed_char         signed char (int8_t on the usual platforms)
  ccol_unsigned_char       unsigned char (uint8_t)
  ccol_short               short
  ccol_unsigned_short      unsigned short
  ccol_int                 int
  ccol_unsigned_int        unsigned int
  ccol_long                long
  ccol_unsigned_long       unsigned long
  ccol_long_long           long long
  ccol_unsigned_long_long  unsigned long long
  ccol_float               float
  ccol_double              double
  ccol_long_double         long double
  ccol_string              char *, signed char *, unsigned char * and
                           their const forms, and an array of any of
                           these character types: a NUL-terminated
                           string whose size includes the NUL
  ccol_pointer             any other object or function pointer, stored
                           as the pointer value itself
  ccol_other_types         a struct, a union, _Bool, and every other
                           type that the list above does not name;
                           such an element is stored, compared and
                           hashed as its bytes

A plain char has its own enumerator, apart from signed char and unsigned
char, because whether a plain char is signed is up to the platform.
signed char has its own one so that its comparison is signed on every
platform.

The fixed-width integer types of <stdint.h> map to the enumerator of the
standard type that the platform defines them as. int64_t, for example,
is ccol_long on LP64 Linux and ccol_long_long on i386. An enum type maps
to the integer type that the compiler makes it compatible with.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
data   The expression whose type the macro looks at. It is never
       evaluated, so an rvalue or a variable that holds no value yet
       is fine.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The ccol_data_type enumerator that matches the type of data, as given
in the table above, or ccol_other_types for any type that the table does
not name.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro is a GNU statement expression, so a translation unit that uses
it needs GCC or Clang with the GNU extensions (-std=gnu11, for example).
Merely including common.h does not need them.

A character pointer always means a NUL-terminated string. For a key made
of binary bytes, use a fixed-size struct (ccol_other_types), or the raw
*_elem functions with an explicit size in the cmap_pair.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <stdint.h>
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

int main(void) {
  char *err = NULL;
  uint64_t key_sample = 0;
  const char *val_sample = NULL;
  chmap m = chmap_create(16,
                         ccol_determine_ccol_data_type(key_sample),
                         ccol_determine_ccol_data_type(val_sample),
                         &err);
  if (!m)
    return 1;
  chmap_destroy(m);
  return 0;
}
.fi
